Build Specification — App 20: Provenance-Aware Annotation Database
Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation. Formatted for copy-paste into another conversation window. No tables, no boxes.

What It Is
A server-side database for annotation collections. It holds the five linked registries — node, event, partition, model, revision — plus a domain-annotation table, and serves annotations over an API. It takes in STAG records and puts out stored records, query results, and API responses. It is for labs and consortia.

Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Store STAG annotation records in a structured database.

Maintain the five linked registries: node, event, partition, model, revision.

Maintain a domain-annotation table linked to the node and partition registries.

Enforce the STAG schema and the thirty-two semantic rules on every stored record.

Enforce referential integrity between the registries.

Enforce supersession chain acyclicity.

Track provenance for every record: who created it, when, from what source, and with what tool.

Support versioned records: each record has a version number and a revision chain.

Support querying by node ID, event node ID, annotation ID, partition ID, partition version, domain ID, model reference, theory version, annotator, date range, display label, evidential anchoring status, and flag.

Support full-text search of the evidence and uncertainty fields.

Support aggregation queries: count by label, count by anchoring status, count by partition, count by domain.

Support exporting a record as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing a record from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface for browsing and querying.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic: same inputs produce same outputs.

Fast: query a typical record in under 100 milliseconds; aggregate over 10,000 records in under one second.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 records.

Versioned: the database records the STAG schema version and the database version.

Reproducible: the database records the provenance for every record.

Portable: works on Linux, macOS, and Windows.

Well-documented: API documentation and schema documentation.

Input Specification
Input 1. STAG annotation record. Required for create and update operations. A JSON object conforming to the STAG schema (Artifact 2, Appendix B.1).

Input 2. Provenance record. Required for create and update operations. The annotator ID, the timestamp, the source, and the tool version.

Input 3. Query. Required for read operations. A query object with fields for node ID, event node ID, annotation ID, partition ID, partition version, domain ID, model reference, theory version, annotator, date range, display label, evidential anchoring status, and flag.

Input 4. Aggregation query. Required for aggregation operations. A query object with fields for the aggregation dimension and the filter.

Input 5. Authentication credentials. Required for authenticated operations.

Input 6. Bulk import file. Optional. A file containing multiple STAG records in JSON or JSON Lines format.

Input 7. Bulk export query. Optional. A query object selecting the records to export.

Output Specification
Output 1. STAG record. JSON. The record as stored.

Output 2. PROV-O serialization. Turtle or JSON-LD. The record mapped to PROV-O (see App 3).

Output 3. Turtle serialization. Turtle. The record as an RDF graph.

Output 4. Query result. JSON. The list of records matching the query, with pagination.

Output 5. Aggregation result. JSON. The counts by the aggregation dimension.

Output 6. Bulk export file. JSON or JSON Lines. The records matching the bulk export query.

Output 7. Audit log entry. JSON. The operation, the record ID, the annotator, the timestamp, and the result.

Output 8. API response. JSON. The result of the API operation, with HTTP status.

Output 9. GraphQL response. JSON. The result of the GraphQL query.

Output 10. Backup file. A database dump or a JSON archive.

Module-by-Module Specification
Module 1: Database Schema
The database has the following tables:

node_registry. Holds the node records. Fields: node_id (primary key), system_scope, model, granularity, partition_id, partition_version, created_at, updated_at.

event_registry. Holds the event node records. Fields: event_node_id (primary key), composition_kind, source_nodes, target_nodes, created_at, updated_at.

partition_registry. Holds the partition records. Fields: partition_id, partition_version (composite primary key), partition_kind, partition_admissibility_status, partition_preregistration_date, partition_justification, intra_domain_rate, created_at, updated_at.

model_registry. Holds the model records. Fields: model_id (primary key), model_reference, theory_version, model_metadata, created_at, updated_at.

revision_registry. Holds the revision records. Fields: revision_id (primary key), annotation_id, supersedes, superseded_by, revision_date, theory_version, annotator_id, created_at.

domain_annotation. Holds the domain annotations. Fields: domain_annotation_id (primary key), node_id (foreign key to node_registry), partition_id, partition_version (foreign key to partition_registry), domain_id, role_tags, formalism, decomposition_source, validity, status, created_at, updated_at.

annotation. Holds the full STAG annotation records. Fields: annotation_id (primary key), node_id (foreign key to node_registry), event_node_id (foreign key to event_registry, nullable), partition_id, partition_version (foreign key to partition_registry), model_id (foreign key to model_registry), revision_id (foreign key to revision_registry), record_json (the full JSON record), derived_display_label, evidential_anchoring_status, unclassified_reason, not_determinable_flag, provisional_flag, threshold_sensitive_flag, regime_not_determinable, identity_partition_sensitive, created_at, updated_at.

provenance. Holds the provenance records. Fields: provenance_id (primary key), annotation_id (foreign key to annotation), annotator_id, annotator_affiliation, timestamp, source, tool_version, operation (create, update, delete), created_at.

audit_log. Holds the audit log entries. Fields: audit_id (primary key), operation, record_type, record_id, annotator_id, timestamp, result, details, created_at.

Module 2: Record Loader
Reads a STAG annotation record from a JSON file or an API request. Validates it against the STAG JSON Schema and the thirty-two semantic rules. Rejects malformed records with an error.

Module 3: Record Writer
Writes a STAG annotation record to the database. Inserts into the node_registry, event_registry, partition_registry, model_registry, revision_registry, domain_annotation, annotation, and provenance tables. Enforces referential integrity.

Module 4: Record Reader
Reads a STAG annotation record from the database. Joins the tables to reconstruct the full record. Returns the record as JSON.

Module 5: Record Updater
Updates a STAG annotation record. Creates a new revision in the revision_registry. Inserts a new provenance record. Updates the annotation table.

Module 6: Record Deleter
Deletes a STAG annotation record. Marks it as deleted (soft delete) or removes it (hard delete, based on configuration). Inserts an audit log entry.

Module 7: Query Engine
Executes queries against the database. Supports queries by node ID, event node ID, annotation ID, partition ID, partition version, domain ID, model reference, theory version, annotator, date range, display label, evidential anchoring status, and flag. Supports pagination.

Module 8: Full-Text Search Engine
Executes full-text search against the evidence and uncertainty fields. Uses the database's full-text search capabilities (PostgreSQL tsvector, SQLite FTS5, or a dedicated search engine).

Module 9: Aggregation Engine
Executes aggregation queries. Supports counts by label, anchoring status, partition, domain, annotator, and date range.

Module 10: Exporter
Exports a record as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle. Uses the PROV-O exporter (App 3) for the PROV-O and Turtle formats.

Module 11: Importer
Imports a record from JSON. Uses the record loader and the record writer.

Module 12: Bulk Import and Export
Imports multiple records from a JSON or JSON Lines file. Exports multiple records to a JSON or JSON Lines file.

Module 13: REST API
Provides the REST API. Endpoints:

GET /records. List records with pagination and filters.

GET /records/{id}. Get a record by ID.

POST /records. Create a record.

PUT /records/{id}. Update a record.

DELETE /records/{id}. Delete a record.

GET /records/{id}/provo. Get a record as PROV-O.

GET /records/{id}/turtle. Get a record as Turtle.

GET /nodes. List nodes.

GET /nodes/{id}. Get a node by ID.

GET /events. List event nodes.

GET /events/{id}. Get an event node by ID.

GET /partitions. List partitions.

GET /partitions/{id}/{version}. Get a partition by ID and version.

GET /models. List models.

GET /models/{id}. Get a model by ID.

GET /revisions. List revisions.

GET /revisions/{id}. Get a revision by ID.

GET /domains. List domain annotations.

GET /domains/{id}. Get a domain annotation by ID.

POST /query. Execute a query.

POST /aggregate. Execute an aggregation query.

POST /search. Execute a full-text search.

POST /bulk/import. Bulk import records.

POST /bulk/export. Bulk export records.

GET /audit. List audit log entries.

Module 14: GraphQL API
Provides the GraphQL API. The schema exposes the same entities as the REST API, with the ability to traverse the relationships between them.

Module 15: Command-Line Interface
Provides the command-line interface for administration:

db init. Initialize the database.

db migrate. Run migrations.

db backup. Backup the database.

db restore. Restore the database.

db import. Import records.

db export. Export records.

db query. Execute a query.

db aggregate. Execute an aggregation query.

db audit. List audit log entries.

Module 16: Authentication and Authorization
Provides authentication (API keys, OAuth, or JWT) and authorization (role-based access control: reader, writer, admin).

Module 17: Audit Logging
Logs every operation (create, update, delete, query) with the annotator, the timestamp, the result, and the details.

Module 18: Backup and Restore
Provides backup and restore. Backup produces a database dump or a JSON archive. Restore loads the backup.

Module 19: Web Interface
Provides a web interface for browsing and querying. Displays records, nodes, events, partitions, models, revisions, and domain annotations. Provides a search box, a filter panel, and an aggregation view.

Module 20: Migration Manager
Manages database migrations. Each schema version has a migration script. Migrations are applied in order.

Full Database Specification
6.1 Node Registry
The node_registry table holds the node records. Each node record has:

node_id (string, primary key)

system_scope (string)

model (string)

granularity (string)

partition_id (string)

partition_version (string)

created_at (ISO8601)

updated_at (ISO8601)

The node_id is a unique identifier. The system_scope, model, and granularity are the three components of the node tuple.

6.2 Event Registry
The event_registry table holds the event node records. Each event node record has:

event_node_id (string, primary key)

composition_kind (string)

source_nodes (array of strings)

target_nodes (array of strings)

created_at (ISO8601)

updated_at (ISO8601)

6.3 Partition Registry
The partition_registry table holds the partition records. Each partition record has:

partition_id (string)

partition_version (string, composite primary key with partition_id)

partition_kind (decompositional, framework_level)

partition_admissibility_status (string)

partition_preregistration_date (ISO8601)

partition_justification (string)

intra_domain_rate (number)

created_at (ISO8601)

updated_at (ISO8601)

6.4 Model Registry
The model_registry table holds the model records. Each model record has:

model_id (string, primary key)

model_reference (string)

theory_version (string)

model_metadata (JSON)

created_at (ISO8601)

updated_at (ISO8601)

6.5 Revision Registry
The revision_registry table holds the revision records. Each revision record has:

revision_id (string, primary key)

annotation_id (string)

supersedes (string or null)

superseded_by (string or null)

revision_date (ISO8601)

theory_version (string)

annotator_id (string)

created_at (ISO8601)

The supersedes and superseded_by fields form the revision chain. The chain must be acyclic.

6.6 Domain Annotation
The domain_annotation table holds the domain annotations. Each domain annotation has:

domain_annotation_id (string, primary key)

node_id (string, foreign key to node_registry)

partition_id (string)

partition_version (string, foreign key to partition_registry)

domain_id (string)

role_tags (array of strings)

formalism (string)

decomposition_source (string)

validity (string)

status (string)

created_at (ISO8601)

updated_at (ISO8601)

6.7 Annotation
The annotation table holds the full STAG annotation records. Each annotation has:

annotation_id (string, primary key)

node_id (string, foreign key to node_registry)

event_node_id (string, foreign key to event_registry, nullable)

partition_id (string)

partition_version (string, foreign key to partition_registry)

model_id (string, foreign key to model_registry)

revision_id (string, foreign key to revision_registry)

